Theme fix: author byline image is missing alt text
===================================================

File: cfi-kadence-child theme, the template part that renders <div class="cfi-byline">
      (search the child theme for: assets/img/aaron.jpg)

The one image on the page that carries an E-E-A-T signal ships with an empty alt
attribute. Everything else in the byline block is correct; only the alt changes.

--- a/cfi-byline.php
+++ b/cfi-byline.php
@@
 <div class="cfi-byline">
 	<img src="https://californiafloodinsurance.com/wp-content/themes/cfi-kadence-child/assets/img/aaron.jpg"
-	     alt="" width="80" height="100" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+	     alt="Aaron J. Farmer, licensed California flood insurance specialist"
+	     width="80" height="100" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
 	<p>
 		Reviewed by <b>Aaron J. Farmer</b>, licensed flood specialist
 		<span>CA License #0L75450 &middot; Last reviewed August 14, 2026</span>
 	</p>
 </div>

Note: the diff above is illustrative — the byline may be emitted from
single.php, a template part, or a shortcode. The change itself is a single
attribute, so apply it wherever aaron.jpg is rendered.

Related, same author-identity problem in structured data
--------------------------------------------------------

The Person node in the page's JSON-LD points its image at a Gravatar
*default* avatar (the generic silhouette), not at Aaron's photo:

    "image": {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      "url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/e3cc...?s=96&d=mm&r=g"
    }

The "d=mm" parameter is Gravatar's mystery-man fallback, which means no
Gravatar is registered for the account email. Two ways to fix, either is fine:

  1. Register a Gravatar for the WordPress user account's email address using
     the same headshot as assets/img/aaron.jpg. Rank Math will then emit a
     real image URL with no code change.

  2. Set a local avatar for the user (Rank Math and most avatar plugins allow
     this) so the Person.image resolves to
     /wp-content/themes/cfi-kadence-child/assets/img/aaron.jpg

Option 1 is less code; option 2 keeps the asset first-party and avoids a
third-party request. Either resolves the signal.
